    "narrative": "LawDog enters the June 29 slate with high-ceiling projections for our lead-off and secondary hitters, headlined by **Matt Chapman**, who tops our daily efficiency board at 69.70. **Matt Chapman** benefits from a massive situational bump, drawing a favorable matchup against Eduardo Rodriguez and leveraging a significant BvP advantage (29 PA) combined with a slow opposing delivery that screams stolen base potential. Similarly, **Dylan Beavers** (66.63) is our strongest utilization play; his 16% park factor boost against Burke, S, along with a strong platoon advantage, makes him an essential start despite hitting in the bottom third of the order. While **Geraldo Perdomo** (63.84) remains a primary focal point assuming he holds the #2 spot, our reliance on him and **Taylor Ward** (62.61) is driven by the strategic necessity to chase runs and AVG from the top of the lineup against mid-tier arms like Mahle and Burke, S.\n\nThe most challenging decision today involves **Cal Raleigh** (58.30), who remains in the starting lineup despite being right on the edge of our \"marginal\" scoring threshold. While **Cal Raleigh** provides power, the 6% deduction for the right-on-right platoon matchup against Ryan Johnson forces us to keep a close eye on the official lineup card. Among those left on the pine, **Yainer Diaz** and **Tyler Freeman** were the closest to making the cut, but they were ultimately relegated to the bench because they lack the high-leverage situational factors\u2014such as batting order position or favorable park alignment\u2014that elevated the current active roster. As always, we are monitoring the health of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith** on the IL, as their absence continues to constrain our depth, leaving us thin if any of our active platoon-dependent bats like **Lawrence Butler** or **Luis Arraez** see their matchup variables shift due to late-breaking lineup changes.",
